---
// ─── Fonts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This starter ships a SYSTEM FONT STACK, set in tokens.css. It costs zero
// bytes, never flashes, and looks native everywhere. That is a genuinely good
// default and you should keep it until you have a reason not to.
//
// When you do want a real typeface, self-host it. A hosted font stylesheet is
// a render-blocking request on the critical path, worth roughly a second of
// FCP and LCP on a slow connection. Everything you need is below, commented,
// along with the three ways this silently goes wrong.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// HOW TO TURN IT ON
//
//   1. npm install @fontsource-variable/inter
//   2. Uncomment the import, the FACES constant, and the <style> at the bottom.
//   3. Uncomment the <link rel="preload"> block.
//   4. In tokens.css, put 'Inter' at the front of --font-sans.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// TRAP 1: do not import the package's CSS.
//
// `import '@fontsource-variable/inter'` looks like the obvious move and it is
// wrong. The variable packages declare a RENAMED family: 'Inter Variable', not
// 'Inter'. Every `font-family: 'Inter'` declaration you have silently fails to
// match, and you get a system fallback that looks almost right.
//
// Import the .woff2 itself with Vite's `?url` instead. You get a same-origin,
// content-hashed URL that is also a build-time literal, which is the only
// shape that can be both cache-busted and named in a <link rel="preload">.
// Then write your own @font-face with the REAL family name.
//
// TRAP 2: `format('woff2-variations')` alone breaks the font.
//
// That keyword was dropped from CSS Fonts 4. A user agent that does not
// recognize it must skip the entire `src` line, and falls back silently. Always
// list a plain `format('woff2')` source after it, as below.
//
// TRAP 3: never preload a theme-conditional face.
//
// Declaring an @font-face does NOT fetch it. Per CSS Fonts L4 a fetch starts
// only when the face is selected for an element actually being laid out. So a
// face used only under `html[data-theme="light"]` costs zero bytes for
// everyone else, with no JS and no flash, because BaseLayout sets the theme
// attribute before first paint.
//
// A <link rel="preload"> is unconditional and defeats that entirely: it
// downloads for every visitor whether the theme is active or not. Preload ONLY
// the faces your default view actually renders. Usually that is one.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// import interLatin from '@fontsource-variable/inter/files/inter-latin-wght-normal.woff2?url';

// Standard Google subset range, copied verbatim from the Fontsource CSS so the
// download-gating behaviour matches what a hosted <link> would have done.
// const LATIN =
//   'U+0000-00FF,U+0131,U+0152-0153,U+02BB-02BC,U+02C6,U+02DA,U+02DC,U+0304,U+0308,U+0329,U+2000-206F,U+20AC,U+2122,U+2191,U+2193,U+2212,U+2215,U+FEFF,U+FFFD';

// const FACES = `
// @font-face{font-family:'Inter';font-style:normal;font-display:swap;font-weight:100 900;src:url(${interLatin}) format('woff2-variations'), url(${interLatin}) format('woff2');unicode-range:${LATIN}}
// `.trim();
---

{/*
  crossorigin is REQUIRED on a font preload even when the file is same-origin.
  Fonts are always fetched in CORS mode, so without it the browser treats the
  preloaded response as unusable and downloads the file a second time. The
  symptom is a font that loads correctly and shows up twice in the network tab.

  <link
    rel="preload"
    as="font"
    type="font/woff2"
    href={interLatin}
    crossorigin
  />
*/}

{/* <style is:inline set:html={FACES} /> */}
